---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PDFCompressor from '../components/PDFCompressor.astro';
import SEOContentPDF from '../components/SEOContentPDF.astro';

const SEO_TITLE = "Compress PDF to 200KB — Fast & Secure Free Compressor";
const SEO_DESC = "Reduce your PDF file to exactly 200KB. Perfect for online applications. 100% free, fast, and secure with no server uploads required.";
---

<Layout title={SEO_TITLE} description={SEO_DESC}>
  <style is:global>
    :root {
      --color-lime: #f43f5e;
    }
  </style>

  <section class="relative z-20 pt-20 container-stripe">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <div style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; background: rgba(244, 63, 94, 0.08); border: 1px solid rgba(244, 63, 94, 0.2); border-radius: 100px; padding: 5px 14px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <span style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--color-lime);">Free PDF Compression</span>
      </div>

      <h1 style="font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 8vw, 4.5rem); line-height: 1; margin: 0 0 24px; letter-spacing: -0.05em;">
        Compress PDF <br/>
        <span style="color: var(--color-lime);">to 200KB.</span>
      </h1>
      <p style="color: var(--color-ink-2); font-size: 1.125rem; margin-bottom: 24px; max-width: 500px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; line-height: 1.6;">
        Instantly compress your PDF document to exactly 200KB for portal uploads. Processed 100% securely on your device.
      </p>
    </div>

    <PDFCompressor defaultTargetKB={200} />
  </section>

  <SEOContentPDF toolName="compress" />

  <!-- Comprehensive 200KB PDF Deep Dive & Application Guide -->
  <article style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 80px auto 120px; padding: 48px 24px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); font-family: var(--font-body); line-height: 1.8; color: var(--color-ink-2); text-align: left;">
    
    <h2 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 2.25rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 24px; letter-spacing: -0.03em; line-height: 1.2;">The Complete Guide to Compressing PDF to 200KB for Online Applications</h2>
    
    <p style="margin-bottom: 24px; font-size: 1.125rem;">
      When applying for competitive examinations, university admissions, and public sector employment in India, document compliance is just as critical as academic preparation. Candidates regularly face strict file upload restrictions where institutional portals enforce an exact upper size threshold—most notably the <strong>200KB PDF limit</strong>. Whether you are submitting academic transcripts, category certificates, or professional credentials, staying within this boundary without sacrificing document legibility is vital. 
    </p>

    <!-- Section: Why 200KB Target -->
    <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 16px;">When 200KB Is the Ideal File Size Target</h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Institutional recruitment servers handle millions of applications during narrow submission windows. To prevent server crashes, database degradation, and bandwidth bottlenecks, portal administrators implement rigid payload constraints. While candidate photographs and signatures are generally restricted to 20KB to 50KB in JPG format, formal documents such as educational marksheets, caste validity certificates, income proofs, and disability declarations are almost universally required in PDF format with an upper cap of <strong>200KB</strong>.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
      The 200KB limit represents an intentional balance between system performance and visual fidelity. A 50KB or 100KB budget is frequently too constrained for complex multi-page records or documents featuring official stamps, holograms, and bilingual text. Conversely, uncompressed scanner outputs typically measure between 2MB and 6MB per document, which would quickly choke institutional bandwidth and storage servers. A 200KB target provides approximately <strong>204,800 bytes</strong>—sufficient storage to retain sharp typographical edges and scannable verification seals while remaining lightweight enough for immediate transmission even over 3G and rural broadband connections.
    </p>

    <!-- Key Scenarios Card Grid -->
    <div style="background: var(--color-surface-2); padding: 32px; border-radius: 16px; margin-bottom: 40px; border: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
      <h4 style="font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 20px;">Key Scenarios Where 200KB PDF Is Mandated</h4>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 24px; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));">
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 8px;">
            <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-lime)" stroke-width="2"><path d="M22 10v6M2 10l10-5 10 5-10 5z"/><path d="M6 12v5c3 3 9 3 12 0v-5"/></svg>
            University Admissions
          </strong>
          <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0;">Central and state university counseling portals (DU CSAS, CUET UG/PG, State CETs) require 10th/12th marksheets and migration certificates strictly under 200KB.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 8px;">
            <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-lime)" stroke-width="2"><rect x="2" y="7" width="20" height="14" rx="2" ry="2"/><path d="M16 21V5a2 2 0 0 0-2-2h-4a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16"/></svg>
            National Entrance Tests
          </strong>
          <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0;">GATE GOAPS, NEET PG counseling, and CSIR-UGC NET require category documents, qualifying degree certificates, and PwD forms capped at 200KB.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 8px;">
            <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-lime)" stroke-width="2"><path d="M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"/><polyline points="14 2 14 8 20 8"/><line x1="16" y1="13" x2="8" y2="13"/><line x1="16" y1="17" x2="8" y2="17"/><polyline points="10 9 9 9 8 9"/></svg>
            PSU & Govt Recruitment
          </strong>
          <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0;">State Public Service Commissions (UPPSC, BPSC, MPSC, TNPSC) and PSUs (ONGC, IOCL, BHEL, DRDO) limit academic and experience PDFs to 200KB.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 8px;">
            <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-lime)" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
            Corporate ATS Systems
          </strong>
          <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0;">Applicant Tracking Systems (Workday, Taleo, Darwinbox) reject consolidated resumes and portfolio PDFs exceeding 200KB during screening.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Specific Portals -->
    <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 16px;">Specific Portals Requiring 200KB PDF Uploads</h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Different examination authorities implement distinct document validation parameters. Below are the primary Indian exam and recruitment portals where maintaining a file size under 200KB in PDF format is strictly mandatory:
    </p>

    <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 24px; margin-bottom: 40px;">
      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); padding: 24px; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
        <h4 style="font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;">
          <span style="color: var(--color-lime);">1.</span> GATE GOAPS (Organizing Institute: IISc Bangalore & IITs)
        </h4>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">
          The GATE Online Application Processing System (GOAPS) has some of the most rigorous scrutiny standards in India. Every applicant uploading supporting documents must ensure their files are in <strong>PDF format between 10 KB and 200 KB</strong>. GOAPS strictly enforces this requirement for:
        </p>
        <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 12px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;">
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: var(--color-lime);">✓</span> <span><strong>Category Certificate:</strong> OBC-NCL, EWS, SC, or ST certificates issued by a competent revenue authority (Tehsildar/SDM).</span></li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: var(--color-lime);">✓</span> <span><strong>Disability Documentation:</strong> PwD Certificate or Certificate of Dyslexic Candidate (Form DYS/PwD) signed by an authorized medical board.</span></li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: var(--color-lime);">✓</span> <span><strong>Qualifying Degree Certificate:</strong> Provisional Certificate or a Course Completion Certificate issued by the Head of the Department/Institute.</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9375rem; color: var(--color-ink-3); margin: 0;">
          <em>Warning:</em> Uploading a file even 0.1 KB above 200 KB triggers an automated rejection error: <code>"Uploaded file exceeds 200KB"</code>. If you compress too aggressively and obscure the issuing officer's signature or stamp, your application will be flagged as "Defective" during scrutiny.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); padding: 24px; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
        <h4 style="font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;">
          <span style="color: var(--color-lime);">2.</span> University Admission Portals (CUET UG/PG & DU CSAS)
        </h4>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">
          The National Testing Agency (NTA) for CUET and Delhi University's Common Seat Allocation System (CSAS) have standardized their certificate upload pipelines:
        </p>
        <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;">
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: var(--color-lime);">✓</span> <span><strong>Delhi University CSAS:</strong> Class 10 certificate (serving as date of birth proof), Class 12 marksheet, and caste/EWS certificates must be uploaded in PDF or JPG format between 100KB and 200KB.</span></li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: var(--color-lime);">✓</span> <span><strong>CUET UG & PG (NTA):</strong> Quota certificates (Sports, ECA, Ward of Kashmiri Migrants, CW) and academic declarations must strictly fall within 50KB to 200KB.</span></li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: var(--color-lime);">✓</span> <span><strong>State Counseling (MHT-CET, WBJEE, KEA, TNEA):</strong> State counseling bodies require domicile, income certificates, and caste validity dossiers uploaded in PDF format capped at 200KB.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); padding: 24px; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
        <h4 style="font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;">
          <span style="color: var(--color-lime);">3.</span> Government Recruitment & Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs)
        </h4>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;">
          State Public Service Commissions (such as UPPSC in Uttar Pradesh, BPSC in Bihar, MPSC in Maharashtra, and TNPSC in Tamil Nadu) alongside leading central PSUs (ONGC, IOCL, NTPC, BHEL, and DRDO) require candidates to upload comprehensive educational and experience portfolios under 200KB during registration:
        </p>
        <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;">
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: var(--color-lime);">✓</span> <span><strong>Consolidated Engineering Transcripts:</strong> Single PDF file containing semester mark sheets, capped at 200KB.</span></li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: var(--color-lime);">✓</span> <span><strong>Prior Experience & NOC Letters:</strong> Clear letterhead scans from previous employers, required to be under 200KB.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Comparison Matrix Table -->
    <div style="overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <h4 style="font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 16px;">Portal Specification Reference Matrix</h4>
      <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; text-align: left; font-size: 0.9375rem;">
        <thead>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-border-mid); color: var(--color-ink);">
            <th style="padding: 12px 16px;">Portal / Institution</th>
            <th style="padding: 12px 16px;">Document Type</th>
            <th style="padding: 12px 16px;">Allowed Format</th>
            <th style="padding: 12px 16px;">Size Constraint</th>
            <th style="padding: 12px 16px;">Critical Verification Requirement</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">GATE GOAPS</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Category, PwD, Degree Certificate</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-family: var(--font-mono);">PDF only</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-family: var(--font-mono); color: var(--color-lime);">10 KB – 200 KB</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Clear issuing authority seal, bilingual signature, valid financial year</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">DU CSAS</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Class 10th DOB & 12th Marksheet</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-family: var(--font-mono);">PDF / JPG</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-family: var(--font-mono); color: var(--color-lime);">100 KB – 200 KB</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Tabular subject marks, board hologram, and candidate roll number</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">CUET (NTA)</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Quota, EWS, Domicile Certificates</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-family: var(--font-mono);">PDF only</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-family: var(--font-mono); color: var(--color-lime);">50 KB – 200 KB</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Scannable QR code and revenue officer digital certificate marker</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">State PSCs & PSUs</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Degree, Experience Letters, NOC</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-family: var(--font-mono);">PDF only</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-family: var(--font-mono); color: var(--color-lime);">Up to 200 KB</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Official letterhead, authorized signatory stamp, serial numbers</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: 100KB vs 200KB Quality Comparison -->
    <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 16px;">Difference Between 100KB and 200KB Compression Quality</h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      A common question among students is: <em>"If my portal allows up to 200KB, should I just compress the PDF to 100KB to be safe?"</em> The short answer is <strong>no</strong>. Compressing a document to 100KB when a 200KB ceiling is permitted unnecessarily degrades visual fidelity and increases the probability of scrutiny rejection.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
      Here is a direct technical comparison between 100KB and 200KB PDF compression:
    </p>

    <div style="display: grid; gap: 24px; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); margin-bottom: 40px;">
      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-2); padding: 24px; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
        <div style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; font-weight: 700; color: #ef4444; margin-bottom: 8px; text-transform: uppercase;">100KB PDF Compression</div>
        <h4 style="font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 12px;">Aggressive Compression Threshold</h4>
        <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; font-size: 0.9375rem;">
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: #ef4444;">⚠</span> <span><strong>Resolution:</strong> Constrained to 100–120 DPI for standard A4 pages.</span></li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: #ef4444;">⚠</span> <span><strong>Fine Text:</strong> Small tabular numerals (like grade points and marks) often blur or form compression halos.</span></li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: #ef4444;">⚠</span> <span><strong>Official Seals:</strong> Embossed university stamps and circular government watermarks blur into indistinct dark spots.</span></li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: #ef4444;">⚠</span> <span><strong>QR Codes & Barcodes:</strong> High-density 2D barcodes (such as Aadhaar or DigiLocker QR codes) frequently fail automated scanner decoding.</span></li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: #ef4444;">⚠</span> <span><strong>Multi-Page Limit:</strong> Highly impractical for documents exceeding 2 pages without severe loss of legibility.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-2); padding: 24px; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--color-lime);">
        <div style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-lime); margin-bottom: 8px; text-transform: uppercase;">200KB PDF Compression (Sweet Spot)</div>
        <h4 style="font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 12px;">Balanced High-Fidelity Retention</h4>
        <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; font-size: 0.9375rem;">
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: var(--color-lime);">✓</span> <span><strong>Resolution:</strong> Retains a crisp 150–200 DPI across all standard A4 dimensions.</span></li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: var(--color-lime);">✓</span> <span><strong>Fine Text:</strong> Font glyphs, fractions, registration numbers, and dates remain perfectly legible.</span></li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: var(--color-lime);">✓</span> <span><strong>Official Seals:</strong> Signatures, purple/blue ink stamps, and state emblems retain authentic edge contrast.</span></li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: var(--color-lime);">✓</span> <span><strong>QR Codes & Barcodes:</strong> Verification matrix remains 100% scannable by phone cameras and OCR verification bots.</span></li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 8px;"><span style="color: var(--color-lime);">✓</span> <span><strong>Multi-Page Limit:</strong> Accommodates 3 to 4 pages comfortably with optimized per-page compression.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Best Practices for Scanning -->
    <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 16px;">Best Practices for Scanning Documents to Keep File Sizes Under 200KB</h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Achieving superior readability at 200KB begins at the capture stage. Most applicants use mobile scanning apps such as Adobe Scan, Microsoft Lens, or CamScanner, which default to 300+ DPI full-color photography. An uncompressed scan from a modern smartphone camera can easily measure 4MB to 8MB. Following these scanning best practices ensures your raw file starts with high clarity and manageable size before compression:
    </p>

    <ol style="padding-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 40px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px;">
      <li>
        <strong style="color: var(--color-ink);">1. Set Scanner DPI to 150 or 200 (Avoid 300+ DPI):</strong>
        Scanning a standard textual marksheet at 300 or 600 DPI is excessive for web portals. A resolution of 150 to 200 DPI produces crisp letterforms and readable stamps while reducing raw uncompressed pixel data by up to 75%.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong style="color: var(--color-ink);">2. Choose Grayscale Over Full 24-Bit Color When Permissible:</strong>
        If your certificate is printed on white or off-white paper with black text and a single blue/black ink signature, scan in <strong>8-bit Grayscale</strong>. Grayscale eliminates unnecessary RGB color channels, immediately cutting raw file size by 66% without any perceptible drop in typographic sharpness. Reserve 24-bit full color only for documents featuring colored candidate photographs or multi-colored holographic emblems.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong style="color: var(--color-ink);">3. Crop Out Ambient Backgrounds, Desks, and Shadows:</strong>
        Never leave surrounding tabletop surfaces, shadows, or bedsheet textures in your scan. Irrelevant background clutter forces the compression algorithm to encode color gradients, consuming valuable byte space that should be allocated to text readability. Crop tightly along the document's physical borders.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong style="color: var(--color-ink);">4. Ensure Flat, Uniform Lighting:</strong>
        Uneven lighting creates dark shadows that mobile scanning algorithms attempt to binarize, resulting in patchy, blotchy artifacts. Place the document flat under indirect daylight or diffuse indoor light before snapping the photo.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong style="color: var(--color-ink);">5. Avoid Pure 1-Bit Binary/Monochrome Thresholding:</strong>
        Some mobile scanner "Magic Color" or "B&W" filters convert images into pure 1-bit black-and-white. While file size becomes tiny (often under 50KB), faint signatures, light rubber stamps, or official watermarks can vanish completely. Stick to grayscale or true color for official submissions.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <!-- Section: How to Compress Multi-Page PDFs -->
    <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 16px;">How to Compress Multi-Page PDFs to Under 200KB</h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Multi-page documents—such as consolidated semester marksheets, 3-year degree transcripts, or comprehensive caste validity dossiers—present an engineering challenge under a 200KB ceiling. The total 200KB data budget must be partitioned among all pages, along with the PDF container's font tables, catalog dictionaries, and metadata.
    </p>

    <div style="background: var(--color-surface-2); padding: 28px; border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 32px; border: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
      <h4 style="font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 16px;">Per-Page Byte Budget Breakdown for 200KB</h4>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 16px; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));">
        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-3); padding: 16px; border-radius: 8px;">
          <span style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 700;">1-PAGE DOCUMENT</span>
          <p style="font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 4px 0;">~190 KB / page</p>
          <span style="font-size: 0.8125rem;">Maximum quality, 200 DPI, full color with intact QR codes.</span>
        </div>
        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-3); padding: 16px; border-radius: 8px;">
          <span style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 700;">2-PAGE DOCUMENT</span>
          <p style="font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 4px 0;">~95 KB / page</p>
          <span style="font-size: 0.8125rem;">Excellent clarity, 175 DPI, readable signatures on both sheets.</span>
        </div>
        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-3); padding: 16px; border-radius: 8px;">
          <span style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 700;">3-PAGE DOCUMENT</span>
          <p style="font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 4px 0;">~62 KB / page</p>
          <span style="font-size: 0.8125rem;">Good clarity, 150 DPI, grayscale recommended for text pages.</span>
        </div>
        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-3); padding: 16px; border-radius: 8px;">
